WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.438
Preference optimization usually asks one objective to supervise an entire answer.

2
00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:13.286
TFPO starts from a different premise: different tokens play different roles, so they should not all receive the same training signal.

3
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:19.264
Reasoning tokens determine whether an explanation is helpful and well justified.

4
00:00:19.264 --> 00:00:27.924
Final answers, option letters, schema keys, and code fragments often need likelihood anchoring to remain stable and parseable.

5
00:00:27.924 --> 00:00:33.401
Applying one sequence-level preference signal everywhere can make these roles interfere.

6
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:38.127
TFPO adds a lightweight gate to every response token.

7
00:00:38.127 --> 00:00:42.715
Low gate values send more weight to a DPO-style preference objective.

8
00:00:42.715 --> 00:00:45.988
High values emphasize chosen-response likelihood.

9
00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:53.656
In effect, reasoning-sensitive spans receive preference shaping, while answer-bearing spans receive stronger anchoring.

10
00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:59.611
The route is learned directly from preference pairs, without token-level supervision.

11
00:00:59.611 --> 00:01:11.029
Three regularizers keep the gate useful: a ratio target prevents branch collapse, total variation discourages noisy switching, and entropy prevents premature saturation.

12
00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:28.313
Under one frozen ten-benchmark protocol, TFPO averages eighty-eight point zero zero on post-trained Qwen three eight B. SimPO reaches eighty-three point zero eight, and the full-response SimPO plus NLL control reaches eighty-three point five two.

13
00:01:28.313 --> 00:01:32.591
TFPO is strongest on every benchmark in the main table.

14
00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:37.598
The routing claim is tested independently of benchmark accuracy.

15
00:01:37.598 --> 00:01:49.905
On one thousand blind annotated responses, the content gate reaches zero point eight five A U P R C, versus zero point five five for the strongest strict position-only control.

16
00:01:49.905 --> 00:01:56.876
Moving the answer to the beginning, middle, or end changes TFPO's score by only zero point zero two.

17
00:01:57.683 --> 00:02:04.797
The same routing recipe improves open-ended alignment across Qwen, Llama, and Mistral backbones.

18
00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:15.803
TFPO is strongest in every reported AlpacaEval and Arena-Hard column, suggesting that the gain is not tied to one tokenizer or one instruction-tuning recipe.

19
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:25.850
With a Qwen three V L backbone, TFPO reaches the best multimodal average of seventy-one point zero three across ten benchmarks.

20
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:38.633
The qualitative cases show three distinct instabilities: omitting a required option letter, selecting an unsupported answer from a map, and producing an unnecessarily long numerical conclusion.

21
00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:52.266
Across four matched samples per prompt, TFPO raises answer agreement to ninety-two point six nine, majority accuracy to eighty-six point three two, and average accuracy to ninety point five seven.

22
00:02:52.266 --> 00:02:59.019
Explanation diversity also rises to sixty-two point three three rather than being traded away.

23
00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:01.743
The central lesson is simple.

24
00:03:01.743 --> 00:03:04.887
It is not enough to ask which objectives should be mixed.

25
00:03:04.887 --> 00:03:08.908
We should also ask where each objective should act inside the response.

26
00:03:08.908 --> 00:03:13.367
TFPO turns that question into a learned, token-level route.
